    labelled(&mut d, "section", "sec:glossary", "Glossary");
    blk(&mut d, "p", "Each term is defined where it is first used in the text. The same definitions, with the field each comes from and what each excludes, are collected here.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:common mycorrhizal network}. A set of fungal threads that colonise the roots of two or more plants at once and so may link them below ground. Field: forest ecology and mycology. Excludes claims about what such a network does, such as moving carbon or signals between trees, which are the matters in dispute.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:focal study}. One of the 18 field studies on these networks whose later citations the original authors read and coded, 11 on network function and 7 on network structure. Field: our usage, following Karst and colleagues. Excludes the citing papers, which are the units counted inside each focal study.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:unsupported citation}. A citation that credits a focal study with a claim that the original authors judged the study's results do not support. Field: citation analysis, as coded by Karst and colleagues. Excludes any judgement of ours: every coding in this paper is the original authors'.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:preregistration}. A dated written plan for a study, fixed and hash-locked before any result is seen: the question, the data, the statistic, the controls, the forecasts and the kill conditions. Field: research method (metascience). Excludes a plan revised after looking at the outcome, and a protocol written only for the record after the run.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:verdict}. The pass or fail of one preregistered rule set against the run's numbers, read off by code. Field: research method (our usage). Excludes an interpretation written after reading the numbers.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:kill condition}. A rule written in the preregistration that, when met, decides against the claim with no room for judgement after the run. Field: research method (our usage, after Popper's falsification). Excludes reasons found after the run for setting a result aside.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:study-year rate}. The share of one focal study's citations in one calendar year that were coded unsupported, as stored in the deposit without its denominator. Field: our usage for the deposited table. Excludes a count of citations, which the deposit does not hold.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:rebuilt denominator}. The number of citations a focal study received in a year, recovered by finding the whole number n for which a published count k gives the deposited rate k over n. Field: our usage in this study. Excludes a count read directly from any source; years with a rate of zero share what remains of the study's total and are the least certain.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:negative binomial model}. A regression for counts whose variance can exceed their mean, fitted here with a log link so that the fitted rate changes by a fixed factor per year. Field: statistics (count regression). Excludes models that weight each year by how many citations it holds.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:within-study model}. A regression with its own baseline level for each focal study, so the year slope is estimated only from change inside studies. Field: statistics (fixed effects). Excludes change that comes from which focal studies happen to be cited in early and late years.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:quasi-Poisson fit}. A log-linear count regression whose standard errors are scaled by the observed spread, used here for the within-study model. Field: statistics (count regression). Excludes a full probability model for the counts.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:citation-weighted share model}. A regression of the number of unsupported citations out of all citations in each study-year on the year, so a year with thirty citations counts thirty times as much as a year with one. Field: statistics (logistic regression). Excludes the equal weight per study-year that a model of the deposited percentages gives.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:study resampling}. Drawing focal studies with replacement, refitting the model each time, and taking the middle 95 percent of the refitted ratios as the interval. Field: statistics (cluster bootstrap). Excludes resampling single study-years, which would ignore that citations of one study move together.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:control}. A version of the data with the structure under test removed (for example shuffled or pooled), scored the same way as the real data, so the real score has something to beat. Field: statistics and experimental design. Excludes a comparison with a different dataset or with a published number.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:p value (from draws)}. The share of control draws scoring at or above the real score, with one added to the count and to the number of draws, so it is never zero. Field: statistics. Excludes a p value from a textbook formula that assumes a distribution.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:planted check}. Data built so that the right answer is known, run through the same analysis before the real data, to show that the analysis can see the effect when it is there. Field: research method; called a positive control in experimental science. Excludes any check made on the real data, which cannot show what the analysis misses.");
    blk(&mut d, "li", "{b:statistical power}. The chance that a test detects an effect of a given size when that effect is real, measured here on planted data with a known doubling. Field: statistics. Excludes the chance that an effect is real given that the test detected it.");
    blk(&mut d, "section", "References");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "karst2023", "Karst, J., Jones, M. D. and Hoeksema, J. D. (2023). Positive citation bias and overinterpreted results lead to misinformation on common mycorrhizal networks in forests. Nature Ecology and Evolution 7, 501-511. doi:10.1038/s41559-023-01986-1");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "karst2023corr", "Karst, J., Jones, M. D. and Hoeksema, J. D. (2023). Author Correction: Positive citation bias and overinterpreted results lead to misinformation on common mycorrhizal networks in forests. Nature Ecology and Evolution 7, 623. doi:10.1038/s41559-023-02035-7");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "dataverse", "Karst, J., Jones, M. D. and Hoeksema, J. D. Deposited rate table (Data_submit.tab) and metadata accompanying Karst et al. (2023). Dataverse, CC0. doi:10.5683/SP3/88MZYX");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "klein2023", "Klein, T., Rog, I., Livne-Luzon, S., van der Heijden, M. G. A. and Körner, C. (2023). Belowground carbon transfer across mycorrhizal networks among trees: facts, not fantasy. Open Research Europe 3, 168. doi:10.12688/openreseurope.16594.1");
    labelled(&mut d, "bib", "simard2025", "Simard, S. W., Ryan, T. S. L. and Perry, D. A. (2025). Opinion: Response to questions about common mycorrhizal networks. Frontiers in Forests and Global Change 7, 1512518. doi:10.3389/ffgc.2024.1512518");
    let ok = save(&d, "F149.doc.tsv");
    println!("{} blocks ({})", d.blocks, ok);
}
